Now I understand the issue. The `decode` method of `UnsignedIntegerCoder` handles `_Unsigned == "true"` for signed integer data (kind "i"), converting to unsigned. But it doesn't handle `_Unsigned == "false"` for unsigned integer data (kind "u"), which is the OPeNDAP/pydap hack where signed bytes are stored as unsigned bytes with `_Unsigned=False`.

The fix: when `data.dtype.kind == "u"` and `unsigned == "false"`, convert unsigned data to signed dtype.

